{"type":"assistant","uuid":"2c17d90a-60f1-47c8-9a83-0110b75e73b8","session_id":"f8ad6213-dd71-4f7d-a5ad-dee030d6bfac","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"2c17d90a-60f1-47c8-9a83-0110b75e73b8","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both places use `.split(\"=\")` without a limit. Let me fix both by adding a limit of 2 to ensure the array always has at least 2 elements (or 1 if there's no equals sign).\n\nActually wait, let me think about this more carefully:\n\nFor `split(\"=\", 2)`:\n- `\"foo=bar\".split(\"=\", 2)` → `[\"foo\", \"bar\"]` ✓\n- `foo\".split(\"=\", 2)` → `[\"foo\"]` — only 1 element, but this is fine since getQueryName would return \"foo\" correctly. But wait, if there's no `=`, then we shouldn't be in a query with a name... Actually we could have queries like `foo` without an equals sign. In that case `split(\"=\", 2)` returns `[\"foo\"]`, and `[0]` = `\"foo\"` — which is actually the correct behavior (the query name is \"foo\" with no value).\n\n\n- For empty strings, `\"\".split(\"=\", 2)` returns `[\"\"]`, so `[0]` is just an empty string. But I'm wondering if an empty query name could even occur in practice.\n\nTesting with `=?` (query string is `?`), the split produces `[\"?\"]`, which gives an empty name and value \"?\" — not great but workable. The real issue I need to handle is the case where someone enters `?=` as the query, which splits to an empty array, so I should default the name to an empty string when that happens.\n\nUsing `split(\"=\", 2)` handles this more robustly since it preserves empty strings properly. Now I need to think through how `getQueryValue` behaves with different inputs and whether there are edge cases I'm missing.\n\nFor `getQueryValue`, when I split on \"=\", it returns different results depending on the input: `[\"foo\", \"bar\"]` for \"foo=bar\", `[\"foo\"]` for just \"foo\", and `[]` for \"=\" with no limit. The current code already checks if the split length is 1 and returns an empty string in that case, so it handles the single-element scenario correctly.\n\nWith the fix using `split(\"=\", 2)`, it returns `[\"\", \"\"]` for \"=\", giving me an empty name and empty value after URL decoding. That works. And for just \"foo\", the split returns `[\"foo\"]`, so I check the length and return an empty string as the value. This approach handles all the edge cases properly.\n\nNow I'll apply both fixes.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
