{"type":"assistant","uuid":"4cd28735-60fd-4446-a271-7cca8645fda6","session_id":"f8ad6213-dd71-4f7d-a5ad-dee030d6bfac","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"4cd28735-60fd-4446-a271-7cca8645fda6","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe fix is complete and verified. Here's the summary:\n\n---\n\n## Bug Fix Summary\n\n### Root Cause\nIn `RemoveQueriesModule.java`, the `UrlParts` inner class uses `String.split(\"=\")` to parse query strings. Java's `split()` method without a limit argument **discards trailing empty strings**. \n\nFor a URL like `https://example.com?=`:\n- The query string is `=`\n- `\"=\".split(\"=\")` returns an **empty array** `[]` (both parts are empty strings, which are trimmed)\n- Accessing `[0]` on this empty array throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`\n\n### Fix Applied\nChanged two `split(\"=\")` calls to `split(\"=\", 2)` — the limit parameter of `2` ensures the returned array always preserves trailing empty strings:\n\n| Query | `\"=\".split(\"=\")` | `\"=\".split(\"=\", 2)` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `=` | `[]` (length 0) → crash | `[\"\", \"\"]` (length 2) → safe |\n| `foo=bar` | `[\"foo\", \"bar\"]` | `[\"foo\", \"bar\"]` |\n| `foo` | `[\"foo\"]` | `[\"foo\"]` |\n\n### Files Changed\n- `app/src/main/java/com/trianguloy/urlchecker/modules/list/RemoveQueriesModule.java`\n\n### Patch (exact changes):\n```diff\n--- a/RemoveQueriesModule.java\n+++ b/RemoveQueriesModule.java\n@@ -188,12 +188,12 @@\n          */\n         public String getQueryName(int index) {\n-            return queries.get(index).split(\"=\")[0];\n+            return queries.get(index).split(\"=\", 2)[0];\n         }\n \n         /**\n          * Returns the decoded value of a query (by index)\n          */\n         public String getQueryValue(int index) {\n-            String[] split = queries.get(index).split(\"=\");\n+            String[] split = queries.get(index).split(\"=\", 2);\n             if (split.length == 1) return \"\";\n```\n\n### Verification\nA standalone Java test confirmed the fix — the buggy code crashes with `IndexOutOfBoundsException` on `\"=\".split(\"=\")`, while the fixed code with `split(\"=\", 2)` handles all edge cases correctly without any exceptions.\n\n### Build Note\nThe Android build (`./gradlew clean assembleDebug`) cannot complete in this environment due to network restrictions preventing download of the Android Gradle Plugin 8.3.0 from Google's Maven repository. The code change itself is syntactically correct and follows the existing project patterns."}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":37491,"output_tokens":724,"cache_read_input_tokens":33536,"total_tokens":38215}}}
